Let me instead point out to those who are interested a good, popularly
written discussion (which nevertheless features a rigorous rebuttal of
these hypotheses) on the subject: the 3rd chapter of Steven Pinker's
_The Language Instinct_. (For a more formal discussion of the
mechanisms of language there are other texts, but you usually don't
want to read these unless you have serious interest and/or spare time.)

BTW, Whorf, whom you seem to cite above, didn't actually know anything
about the cultures he "studied". He was an insurance inspector (which
doesn't prevent him from making linguistic discoveries per se, of
course) with an interest in linguistics. His "insight" came initially
when he inspected cases of workers causing fires by misunderstandings
which, in his time, he could only attribute to language. Nowadays we
have the tools (from cognitive science, theoretical and
neurolinguistics, modern psychology and psycholinguistics, etc.) to
think about thoughts, even though we use language to talk about them,
to perceive (i.e. "import") them, and to express them. There's no need
to attribute genuine stupidity to poor language, for example.
Studies with patients with various brain traumas have shown that
these things are quite separate (i.e. can be affected distinctly by
e.g. brain lesions).

Note that Whorf's arguments don't have any significant academic
support also because he never actually traveled to the cultures he
talked about, made inadequate translations from Apache, Navajo, and
Hopi which he used to support his arguments and wrote in a remarkable
style, "forgetting" to reason his thoughts most of the time. His
relative success at the time was most probably entirely a result of
his maximised personal Cha.

(I know I said I won't spend my time but I can't help it...)

In his book, Pinker summarises most of the points which were available
a decade or more ago. They are far too many to list here, but I will
provide some of the simple, less formal ones:

* Sometimes we can't find the right words for a thought. This means
  that there must be a separate representation or we would be able
  to utter it immediately. Also, when we remember something, we don't
  remember the exact words. This means that the quintessence does not
  lie with them.

* Whorf's famous example with Hopi and their supposed lack of a sense
  of time because of the supposed lack of constructs for it in their
  language is easily refuted with this translated Hopi sentence:

             Then indeed, the following day, quite early
             in the morning at the hour when people pray
             to the sun, around the time then he woke up
             the girl again.
                        (from E. Malotki, cited by Pinker)

  (even though the original sentence contained some import words, many
  time words (like "yesterday") have been found to have local roots.
  The Hopi, of course, also had a calendar system and time-showing
  equipment. Whorf just didn't know any of this and apparently his
  source materials didn't feature words such as "yesterday".)

  To me, anyone who takes this argument seriously has serious
  problems with his concept of time himself. It is not an *optional*
  thing. It is a directed spatiotemporal entity every human being
  needs to conceptualize in order to function and be able to
  communicate about it. Duh.

* Children learning language, deaf and pidgin-speaking people
  recreating their own (even in absence of a full-featured language),
  langueless people being able to think and talk about it later are
  other examples, which, if you take the time to analyse them
  properly, conflict with Whorf's views. (People have done it
  elsewhere.)

* The only "dramatic" finding (as Pinker puts it) in support of Whorf
  came from an experiment which showed that Chinese Students have
  problems understanding counterfactual information presented in a
  specific form compared to Americans, supposedly because Chinese
  lacks a "native" subjunctive construction ("If John were to drink a
  Jack, he would drink another"). As soon as the results came out,
  cognitive psychologists with a better command of Chinese than the
  American experimenters found flaws (ambiguities) in the test. When
  they were corrected, and the test repeated, the differences
  vanished.

BTW one of the popular internet (and non-internet) hoaxes, the one
about Eskimos having a gazillion words for snow also has its roots in
Whorf's "remarkable" approach to science.

As most people know by now, Eskimo is a polysynthetic language. This
means, for example, that in order to express thoughts that would
require a multi-word phrase/sentence in English, an Eskimo might need
only a single, albeit complicated, word. This word "flexibility"
allows one to argue that they have, for instance, a word for "the snow
from this morning that I have laden in my wagon" (as far as I remember
from a past discussion with a linguistics professor).

Well in some sense they do. If you are reasonable and look at the
roots you will find a much smaller number however. Boas (the mentor of
Whorf's teacher, Sapir) counted 4 in 1911 while Whorf "embellished"
that number to 7 and suggested there were more (again citing Pinker,
who cites Laura Martin's report on the issue).  His article was widely
published, his suggestion successfully inflated, and the rest of the
misconceptions is history.

(Of course, today's linguists may all be wrong as, by your reasoning,
Eskimos would be one of the most abstract thinking nations on earth
(their polysynthetic perversity being a world apart form simple German
determinative Composita) and by now would have created labels for the
most abstract snow-related concepts... :-)

You may still want to advocate a weaker version of this hypothesis,
namely that language could *mildly influence* thought. And in this
case let me tell you: I agree, language can influence thought. A very
tiny bit.

The language faculty of human beings is a highly specialised
apparatus, which can perform certain operations very quickly. Sadly,
most of its operation is unconscious. Still, the part that we can, in
some sense, "control" consciously can be used for thinking outside of
what one would usually call language-related behaviour (speaking,
listening, ...). Our mind can simply map some operations to a problem
the performance system of the language faculty can assist in solving.
But the current understanding that this performance system is highly
independent of specific languages leaves very little room for your
arguments. The only example I know is the experiment where it was
found that people could *slightly* better recall colours for which
their native language had a name. But this "recalling" could in fact
be carried out (in part) by the language faculty in redundancy to the
conceptual recalling. Furthermore, experiments have shown that there
is no different "perception" of colours (as suggested by Whorf). For
languages that have a name for say blue, red, and green, test persons
will always pick the same colours representing the most "clean"
version of the colour. This has proven physiological reasons.

So yes, for some elementary things, you may find infinitesimal
differences in performance by speakers of different languages.
Whenever you venture into more complex, "abstract" thought you will
see that specific languages have nothing to do with it.

